  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none noted",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study compares downstream task scores for a same-corpus, same-tokenizer model trained on fewer tokens with the full-training model, providing a limited test of downstream performance versus training-token scale. It does not fit or measure pretraining-loss scaling laws.",
    "gate_note": "The relevant results are the paper's own downstream scores for CamemBERT30% and CamemBERTCCNet, which use the MLM objective and differ in training duration. These measure downstream performance at two training-token amounts, not held-out cross-entropy, fitted power laws, extrapolation, or compute-optimal allocations. CamemBERTa's comparisons with other models change architecture and/or objective as well as training setup, so they are not treated as clean scale comparisons. Cited scaling-law findings are not recorded as results.",
    "test_reason": "The paper introduces CamemBERTa, a French DeBERTaV3 model, and compares architectures and objectives on downstream tasks. It does not fit or measure any scaling relation, and cites Hoffmann et al. only as background. The CamemBERT30% vs CamemBERTCCNet pair is only two points. It is also confounded: CamemBERT30% used the authors' own recipe (LAMB, batch 67,584/27,648, two-phase sequence length), while CamemBERTCCNet used the original CamemBERT recipe. So it cannot show whether downstream performance improves predictably with training tokens (V6).",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely right. The authors conclude that CamemBERTa, trained on about 30% of CamemBERTCCNet's tokens and compute, matches or surpasses it downstream. They also conclude it beats the same-setup MLM model CamemBERT30% on all tasks except FTB NER; the Discussion also excepts POS on Rhapsodie. They attribute the gains to architecture and the RTD objective, not scale.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "No held-out-loss scaling sweep or power-law fitting; the paper compares downstream task results for two training durations, with different pretraining objectives also represented in the broader comparisons.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Downstream result varies by task: the 30%-token model exceeds the full-training reference on NER, while the full-training reference scores higher on the other paired measures recorded.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "An MLM model trained on 133B tokens with a large-batch LAMB recipe (CamemBERT30%) scores below CamemBERTCCNet (419B tokens, original recipe) on most downstream tasks. The gaps are larger on QA and FLUE than on POS tagging and parsing; CamemBERT30% scores higher on FTB NER.",
        "proposed_claim": "More pretraining tokens raise downstream scores of base-size MLM encoders mainly on semantic tasks, with little effect on low-level syntactic tasks (confounded with the training recipe).",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r3",
